-- title:
--   Coefficient obstruction after quadratic polynomial reduction
-- statement:
--   Keep every geometric, contact-ideal, initial-value, scalar ODE, Weierstrass-curve, and numerical hypothesis of the parent. After constructing the four formal coordinate series J, allow any function ρ on the four-variable polynomial ring satisfying, for every p, degree_{X₂}ρ(p)≤1 and p−ρ(p)∈(q), where q is the fixed Weierstrass cubic.
--
--   Choose p,F,a,b,s with the parent's relation and finite coefficient obstruction, using ρ(p) in both the contact quotient and every formal polynomial evaluation. All tested polynomial representatives are therefore at most linear in X₂. The completed theorem supplies such a function, so the forward sketch obtains the parent's witnesses by using ρ(p) itself.
--
--   Conversely, the cubic lies in the contact ideal and vanishes under every formal coordinate evaluation. Every allowed ρ therefore preserves the relevant quotient class and all formal coefficients. A Lean converse checks the same C, subset threshold, F,a,b,s, and weights. The parent has no total-degree or height bound on the witness p; its existing bounds are all retained. The geometric exclusion of the reduced coefficient system remains open.
-- source:
--   Derived quadratic polynomial reduction step for https://prove2.me/theorems/590e44cd-6794-4e85-82c6-06626372866d. Mission context: Senthil Kumar K (2026), Algebraic independence of values of Weierstrass elliptic and zeta functions, Appendix A.2, Theorem A.3 and Proposition A.1; https://doi.org/10.1017/S001309152610145X. This is a derived algebraic tool, not a completion of the zero estimate. Primary Lean sources: Mathlib Algebra/MvPolynomial/Degrees.lean, RingTheory/Ideal/Span.lean and RingTheory/Ideal/Quotient/Defs.lean, revision 0df444a360eaa60ab8c11dca51a86af692955474. Polynomial induction reduces any polynomial to degree at most one in X2 modulo the Weierstrass cubic. The reduction preserves both the contact-quotient class and all evaluations along the formal Weierstrass curve, so the coefficient obstruction and its bounds are unchanged.

